7592186540011 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186539620 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540011 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186539737 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540011 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186539878 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540011 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540011 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540011 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186539737 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540011 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ee07a34b-76bb-5c4b-82db-1a6944f98c6a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540022 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539981 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540022 #edb/ref 1069 "q-107731" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540022 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540022 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/62/6240abf268497f82475003619689d979b0b00678b4d49972ff2fbcf1fa866a9c.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a cubic gas tank. One-fifth of the volume of the tank is filled with gas. If the fuel costs $$0.0016$ per cubic centimeter, how much would it cost to fill the tank to capacity?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540022 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "014f97dd-12f1-54e9-8347-3c53a77026ab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540022 #edb/ref 1074 "The length of $7\\,\\text{cm}$ shown in the diagram represents one-fifth of the tank's side length. Therefore, the length of each side of the tank is\n\n$$\n5\\cdot 7=35\\,\\text{cm}.\n$$\n\nThe empty space in the cube is a rectangular prism that is $35\\,\\text{cm}$ long, $35\\,\\text{cm}$ wide, and $35-7=28\\,\\text{cm}$ high.\n\nWe can find the volume $V$ of the rectangular prism using the formula\n\n$$\nV=lwh,\n$$\n\nwhere $l$ is the length, $w$ is the width, and $h$ is the height of the solid.\n\nSubstituting \n\n$$\nl=35\\,\\text{cm},\\,w=35\\,\\text{cm},\\,h=28\\,\\text{cm}\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we obtain\n\n$$\nV=35\\cdot 35\\cdot 28=34\\,300\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, to fill the tank to capacity, it would cost\n\n$$\n34\\,300\\cdot 0.0016=$54.88.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540030 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539926 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540030 #edb/ref 1069 "q-118846" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540030 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540030 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the volume of a rectangular solid that has length, width, and height of $\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{ft},$ $\\sqrt{10}\\,\\text{ft},$ and $2\\,\\text{ft},$ respectively?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540030 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1e1eb62e-08d0-56cd-999d-56094605f117" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540030 #edb/ref 1074 "We can find the volume $V$ of the rectangular prism using the formula\n\n$$\nV=lwh,\n$$\n\nwhere $l$ is the length, $w$ is the width, and $h$ is the height of the solid.\n\nSubstituting \n\n$$\nl=\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{ft},\\,w=\\sqrt{10}\\,\\text{ft},\\,h=2\\,\\text{ft}\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we obtain\n\n$$\nV=\\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\sqrt{10}\\cdot 2=2\\sqrt{50}=10\\sqrt{2}\\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540046 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539945 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540046 #edb/ref 1069 "q-107606" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540046 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540046 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the volume of a rectangular solid that has length, width, and height of $2\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{m},$ $4\\,\\text{m},$ and $3\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{m},$ respectively?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540046 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3d05a680-5939-5ca9-9607-1df6158bf7b7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540046 #edb/ref 1074 "We can find the volume $V$ of the rectangular prism using the formula\n\n$$\nV=lwh,\n$$\n\nwhere $l$ is the length, $w$ is the width, and $h$ is the height of the solid.\n\nSubstituting \n\n$$\nl=2\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{m},\\,w=4\\,\\text{m},\\,h=3\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{m}\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we obtain\n\n$$\nV=2\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot 3\\sqrt{3}=24\\sqrt{6}\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540047 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539951 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540047 #edb/ref 1069 "q-107630" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540047 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540047 #edb/ref 1071 "A storage center has ten identical storage units in the form of a rectangular solid. Each unit is $9\\,\\text{ft}$ long, $24\\,\\text{ft}$ wide, and $8\\,\\text{ft}$ high. What is the total combined volume of the units?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540047 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3e2801a7-e084-5269-a941-751cceb06faf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540047 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we need to find the volume of one storage unit.\n\nWe can find the volume $V$ of the rectangular prism using the formula\n\n$$\nV=lwh,\n$$\n\nwhere $l$ is the length, $w$ is the width, and $h$ is the height of the solid.\n\nSubstituting \n\n$$\nl=9,\\,w=24,\\,h=8\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V=9\\cdot 24\\cdot 8=1\\,728\\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total volume of the ten storage units is\n\n$$\n1\\,728\\cdot 10=17\\,280\\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540060 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539992 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540060 #edb/ref 1069 "q-107608" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540060 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540060 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the volume of a rectangular solid that has length, width, and height of $9\\,\\text{ft},$ $4\\,\\text{ft},$ and $5\\,\\text{ft},$ respectively?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540060 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "76532f80-93cf-5f33-8bd0-322c7a249f9a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540060 #edb/ref 1074 "We can find the volume $V$ of the rectangular prism using the formula\n\n$$\nV=lwh,\n$$\n\nwhere $l$ is the length, $w$ is the width, and $h$ is the height of the solid.\n\nSubstituting \n\n$$\nl=9\\,\\text{ft},\\,w=4\\,\\text{ft},\\,h=5\\,\\text{ft}\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we obtain\n\n$$\nV=9\\cdot 4\\cdot 5=180\\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540064 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186540011 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540064 #edb/ref 1069 "q-118851" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540064 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540064 #edb/ref 1071 "The height of a rectangular solid is $2\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{m}$ and its volume is $24\\sqrt{3}\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}.$ If the base  (length × width) of the solid is a square, find the length of the solid." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540064 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "78b1f6d8-2df3-5686-9ee0-696c5e38afe8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540064 #edb/ref 1074 "We can find the volume $V$ of the rectangular prism using the formula\n\n$$\nV=lwh,\n$$\n\nwhere $l$ is the length, $w$ is the width, and $h$ is the height of the solid.\n\nSince the base of the rectangular solid is a square, we have that \n\n$$\nl=w.\n$$\n\nSubstituting \n\n$$\nV=24\\sqrt{3},\\,h=2\\sqrt{3},\\,w=l\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =lwh \\\\ V & ={l}^{2}h \\\\ 24\\sqrt{3} & ={l}^{2}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ {l}^{2} & =12 \\\\ l & =2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length is $2\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{m}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540070 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539999 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540070 #edb/ref 1069 "q-10230" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540070 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540070 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/37/37e62bc959e4a71abdeb9efab170916a914d687d5c02e7850d01eb281acd131b.png)\n\nDetermine the height of the rectangular solid above given that its volume is $702\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540070 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "90da9001-fcc8-57a5-93d8-6080245acd79" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540070 #edb/ref 1074 "We can find the volume $V$ of the rectangular prism using the formula\n\n$$\nV=lwh,\n$$\n\nwhere $l$ is the length, $w$ is the width, and $h$ is the height of the solid.\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\nV=702,\\,l=13,\\,w=6\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}702 & =13\\cdot 6\\cdot h \\\\ 702 & =78h \\\\ h & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the height is $9\\,\\text{m}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540071 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539960 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540071 #edb/ref 1069 "q-118872" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540071 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540071 #edb/ref 1071 "A person has seven identical boxes in the form of a rectangular solid. Each box is $10\\,\\text{in}$ long, $8\\,\\text{in}$ wide, and $8\\,\\text{in}$ high. What is the total combined volume of the boxes?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540071 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "90edfdf5-24c3-54cd-8862-63c978f95f47" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540071 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we need to find the volume of one box.\n\nWe can find the volume $V$ of the rectangular prism using the formula\n\n$$\nV=lwh,\n$$\n\nwhere $l$ is the length, $w$ is the width, and $h$ is the height of the solid.\n\nSubstituting \n\n$$\nl=10,\\,w=8,\\,h=8\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V=10\\cdot 8\\cdot 8=640\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total volume of the seven storage boxes is\n\n$$\n640\\cdot 7=4\\,480\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540072 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186540008 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540072 #edb/ref 1069 "q-107620" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540072 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540072 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/ab/abec41d02ba71b2e94d7b2c3cd92f552ddab907de494719a22706b568a588be4.png)\n\nDetermine the length of the rectangular solid above given that its volume is $84\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540072 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "91cc3a07-1501-5243-8832-8d928c7de8f2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540072 #edb/ref 1074 "We can find the volume $V$ of the rectangular prism using the formula\n\n$$\nV=lwh,\n$$\n\nwhere $l$ is the length, $w$ is the width, and $h$ is the height of the solid.\n\nSubstituting \n\n$$\nV=84,\\,w=3\\sqrt{2},\\,h=\\sqrt{8}\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we obtain the following\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}84 & =l\\cdot 3\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{8} \\\\ 84 & =3\\sqrt{16}l \\\\ 84 & =12l \\\\ l & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length is $7\\,\\text{cm}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540088 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539997 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540088 #edb/ref 1069 "q-118847" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540088 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540088 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the volume of a rectangular solid that has length, width, and height of $7\\,\\text{cm},$ $\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{cm},$ and $2\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{cm},$ respectively?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540088 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bc3873ba-541e-5572-9cfe-0e10d2402ed6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540088 #edb/ref 1074 "We can find the volume $V$ of the rectangular prism using the formula\n\n$$\nV=lwh,\n$$\n\nwhere $l$ is the length, $w$ is the width, and $h$ is the height of the solid.\n\nSubstituting \n\n$$\nl=7\\,\\text{cm},\\,w=\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{cm},\\,h=2\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{cm}\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we obtain\n\n$$\nV=7\\cdot \\sqrt{5}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{5}=14\\sqrt{25}=70\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540102 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539930 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540102 #edb/ref 1069 "q-118873" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540102 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540102 #edb/ref 1071 "A shipping company delivered six identical packages in the form of a rectangular prism. Each package is $40\\,\\text{cm}$ long, $20\\,\\text{cm}$ wide, and $10\\,\\text{cm}$ high. What is the total combined volume of the packages?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540102 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e195bf20-65ce-5bd5-85a8-c18879f5cecb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540102 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we need to find the volume of one package.\n\nWe can find the volume $V$ of the rectangular prism using the formula\n\n$$\nV=lwh,\n$$\n\nwhere $l$ is the length, $w$ is the width, and $h$ is the height of the solid.\n\nSubstituting \n\n$$\nl=40,\\,w=20,\\,h=10\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V=40\\cdot 20\\cdot 10=8\\,000\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total volume of the six packages is\n\n$$\n8\\,000\\cdot 6=48\\,000\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
